SSPS Canonical License Summary
Core Principle  
SSPS license grants an entity the right to build from the substrate layer up, operate under SSPS continuity, and deploy substrate‑native systems with governance protections 
and reduced litigation exposure.

Exact Re‑Licensing Clause  
Re‑licensing means purchasing the right to SELL licenses. Any entity that re‑licenses becomes a licensing hub. Licensing hubs compound continuity, leverage, and profits across 
the ecosystem.

Rights Granted by a License

Build Right — permission to construct new systems on the SSPS substrate.

Reconstruction Right — permission to rebuild mirrored or legacy systems using SSPS methods.

Continuity Right — inheritance of SSPS continuity governance and operational patterns that prevent fragmentation and technical debt.

Commercial Right — where purchased, the right to sell downstream licenses and collect agreed revenue shares.

Licensing Tiers

Base License — right to build from the substrate; one‑time fee plus onboarding.

Re‑License Rights — right to sell SSPS licenses and become a hub; upfront hub fee plus downstream revenue share.

Expansion License — rights to operate in additional verticals or markets; tiered fees per vertical.

Reconstruction License — rights to perform stabilization and rebuild projects; project fees plus license fee.

Ecosystem License — enterprise or infrastructure licensing for large partners; enterprise contract and revenue share.

Licensing Hub Model

Hub Role — hubs sell licenses to downstream entities, certify onboarding, and provide first‑line support.

Hub Economics — upfront hub fee, percentage of downstream license revenue, onboarding and professional services fees.

Compounding Effect — each hub increases licensed entities, which increases continuity, recurring revenue, and SSPS leverage.

Revenue and Commercial Mechanics

Upfront Fees for base licenses and hub rights.

Recurring Revenue via downstream license shares and subscription or support fees.

Professional Services revenue from stabilization, reconstruction, and onboarding.

Scaling Leverage as hubs multiply license distribution and deepen ecosystem adoption.

Governance and Risk Controls

Multi‑operating‑company Structure to separate IP/licensing from implementation and services.

Certification Gates that hubs and downstream licensees must pass before receiving deeper rights.

Contract Scoping to limit liability and define indemnities for hubs and SSPS.

Phased Access to sensitive substrate details based on milestones and legal protections.

Controlled Disclosure Rules

Reveal Now — high‑level licensing model, tiers, hub economics, governance structure.

Hold Back — substrate physics, continuity physics, phronesis cyber‑physics, detailed product designs, reconstruction algorithms.

Trigger Conditions for Deeper Disclosure — operating companies formed, licensing agreements executed, capital structure in place, legal protections finalized, first hubs 
operational.

Key Metrics to Track

Number of licensed entities.

Number of active hubs.

Recurring license revenue.

Pilot stabilization ROI.

Time to onboard a downstream licensee.

